module

public import Rule30Open.Columns.WordTraps

/-!
# Traps for 12 more blocks, from the walls of period twelve carried past the depth of `TwelveTraps`

The census of locked windows of 16 cells at periods 10 to 14 sorts every lock by the background its chain ends in. Of
the blocks those chains hold, the trap modules miss 12, all of period 12, which the wall of `001011100111`, at the
edge of the background of 60 cells, reaches at distances 41 to 54, past the 40 columns that `TwelveTraps` carries it.
Here it is carried to each of them, a trap of 119 states and 57 to 70 cells, which the kernel checks one declaration
each.

* `far<period>_<distance>`, `far<period>_<distance>_check`: the traps, each passing the check of
  [WordTraps](WordTraps.md).
* `farWalls`, `farWalls_check`, `farWalls_blocks`: the list, its check and its blocks.
* `far_absorption_excludes`: for each of them, if every finitely supported right half driven by the block enters its
  trap, the center column does not eventually repeat the block.

The tables come from `python -m computations.lock_chain --lean-far`. The natural-language argument is `FarWalls.md`,
beside this file.
-/

@[expose] public section

-- Sequential elaboration: the kernel checks would otherwise run in parallel and stack their memory.
set_option Elab.async false

namespace Rule30Open

